.TH CHMOD 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
chmod \- change file permissions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chmod
.RB [ \-Rcvf ]
.I mode
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B chmod
changes the permissions of each
.I file
according to
.IR mode .
On Windows this sets or clears the read-only attribute; see
.BR Windows ,
below.
.PP
Files are processed in order.
A file that cannot be found or changed is reported on standard error,
and the remaining files are still processed.
.SS Modes
A
.I mode
is octal or symbolic.
.PP
An octal
.I mode
is a number from 0 to 7777, written with octal digits only, that
replaces the whole mode.
It is the sum of 4000 for set-user-ID, 2000 for set-group-ID, 1000 for
sticky, and read 4, write 2, and execute 1, times 100 for the owner, 10
for the group, and 1 for others.
An octal
.I mode
cannot be combined with symbolic clauses.
.PP
A symbolic
.I mode
is a list of clauses separated by commas, applied in turn to the file's
current mode.
A clause is zero or more class letters,
.B u
for the owner,
.B g
for the group,
.B o
for others, and
.B a
for all three, then one or more operations, as in
.BR u+r\-x .
An operation is an operator followed by zero or more permission letters,
or by a single class letter.
The operators are:
.TP
.B +
Add the permissions to the named classes.
.TP
.B \-
Remove the permissions from the named classes.
.TP
.B =
Clear all permissions of the named classes, then add the given ones.
.PP
The permission letters are:
.TP
.B r
Read.
.TP
.B w
Write.
.TP
.B x
Execute.
.TP
.B X
Execute, but only if the file is a directory or has some execute bit
set, counting changes made earlier in the
.IR mode .
.TP
.B s
Set-user-ID with
.BR u ,
set-group-ID with
.BR g ,
and both with
.B a
or no class letters.
.TP
.B t
Sticky, only with
.B a
or no class letters.
.PP
A class letter
.BR u ,
.BR g ,
or
.B o
after the operator stands for the read, write, and execute permissions
that class has at that point, as in
.BR g=u .
.PP
With no class letters, a clause acts as with
.BR a ,
except that it neither adds nor removes the bits set in the umask,
though
.B =
still clears them.
See
.BR BB_UMASK .
An empty clause, or an empty
.IR mode ,
changes nothing.
Any other text, such as a class letter at the end of the
.IR mode ,
makes the
.I mode
invalid.
.SS Windows
Windows files have no Unix mode.
busybox-w32 derives one for each file from its attributes, name, and
contents, as shown by
.BR "ls \-l" ,
and a symbolic
.I mode
is applied to that.
Of the resulting mode,
.B chmod
uses only the owner write bit, 200.
If it is clear,
.B chmod
sets the read-only attribute of
.IR file ,
and if it is set,
.B chmod
clears it.
The other bits are discarded, and other attributes are unchanged.
So
.BR u\-w ,
.BR a\-w ,
and
.B 444
make a file read-only,
.BR u+w ,
.BR a+w ,
and
.B 644
make it writable, and
.B +x
and
.B go\-w
change nothing.
.PP
In the derived mode, a directory has every read, write, and execute bit
set.
Any other file has every read bit, every write bit unless it is
read-only, and every execute bit if it is named with a
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
or
.B .cmd
suffix, in any case, or if its contents begin with
.B #!\&
or are a Windows program other than a DLL.
Group and other write bits that are set in the umask are clear.
.B chmod
cannot change the execute bits.
.PP
A directory never becomes read-only: whatever the
.IR mode ,
.B chmod
clears the read-only attribute of every directory it processes.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-Rv ,
and may follow the operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
A
.I mode
that begins with a dash, such as
.BR \-w ,
is taken as the
.IR mode ,
not as options.
.B \-\-
ends the options, for a
.I file
whose name begins with a dash.
.TP
.B \-R
Recurse: change each
.I file
that is a directory, then everything below it.
Symbolic links and junctions found inside are skipped, and one named as
a
.I file
is not descended into.
A directory whose mode cannot be changed is not descended into.
.TP
.B \-c
For each file whose mode changes, print a line on standard output:
.RS
.PP
.EX
mode of \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq changed to \fIoctal\fR (\fIperms\fR)
.EE
.RE
.IP
where
.I octal
is the new mode as 4 octal digits and
.I perms
is its 9 permission characters, as in the
.B ls \-l
format.
.TP
.B \-v
Print that line for every file, whether or not its mode changes.
.TP
.B \-f
Do not report a file whose mode cannot be changed.
A file that does not exist, a directory that cannot be read, and, with
.BR \-R ,
a directory whose mode cannot be changed are still reported.
The exit status is unaffected.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
The umask, in octal, for clauses with no class letters and for the
derived mode.
The kit's
.B sh
sets and exports it in its
.B umask
builtin.
When it is unset, the umask is 0002.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Variables that busybox-w32 reads in every applet, such as those that
control console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line, with a usage message on standard error; an invalid
.IR mode ;
or a file could not be found or changed.
An invalid
.I mode
is reported at the first
.I file
that exists, and
.B chmod
stops there, before changing anything.
.SH EXAMPLES
Make a file read-only, reporting the change:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ chmod \-v a\-w config.h
mode of \(aqconfig.h\(aq changed to 0444 (r\-\-r\-\-r\-\-)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make it writable again:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ chmod u+w config.h
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make every file in a tree writable, as after copying it from read-only
media:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ chmod \-R u+w project
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make the C sources in a tree read-only:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find src \-name \(aq*.[ch]\(aq \-exec chmod 444 {} +
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU chmod:
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options except
.BR \-\-help ,
and so no
.B \-\-reference
or
.BR \-\-preserve\-root .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-v
reports every file as changed, even when its mode is unchanged.
.PP
.B chmod
does not change a file's owner or its access control list, which is what
usually controls access on Windows.
.PP
.B \-c
and
.B \-v
report the mode computed from the derived one, not what is stored, so
.B "chmod \-c u+x notes.txt"
reports a change that has no effect, and a directory is reported with
the mode given even though it stays writable.
.PP
Scripts that run
.B chmod +x
succeed and change nothing, and a file that begins with
.B #!\&
is already executable.
.PP
.BR lsattr (1)
shows the read-only attribute itself, including that of a directory.
To set the read-only attribute of a directory, or the hidden, system,
and other attributes, use
.BR chattr (1).
.PP
Output lines end with LF, not CRLF.
.I file
may use a drive letter and either kind of slash.
A bare drive such as
.I c:
names the current directory of that drive, and with
.B \-R
the files below it are reported as
.IR c:name .
On Windows 10 version 1903 or later, file names may contain any Unicode
characters, and on older systems only those of the system code page.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR chattr (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR ls (1),
.BR lsattr (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR stat (1)
